{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And he was building a city.</b> It does not say 'וַיִּבֶן' ('and he built') but rather 'וַיְהִי בּוֹנֶה' ('and he was building') — the participle form — to indicate that he kept building all his days and his building was never completed forever. For this is the way of them — folly to them — that everyone who loves the acquisitions of the earth, like Cain and his companions, forever builds on and on and can never complete his building all his days, forever. For forever he lacks nothing that he should lack — he has a hundred and craves two hundred, as is well known from the nature of all lovers of money. And if so, forever he builds on and on, and all his days he has no rest, only 'נָע וָנָד' ('a restless wanderer') on the earth like Cain, who was 'נָע וָנָד' on the earth. So every lover of money is 'נָע וָנָד' — his sitting at home is brief; the majority of his days he pursues wealth like a forager roaming the mountains. And he runs with feet like gazelles in the wilderness, on mountain and in lowland, crossing the routes of the seas all the days of his life — these are the nights: even at night his heart did not lie down. For he goes to all places of danger to attain what his soul loves; he seeks it and does not find it, and never reaches its goal — only all his days he builds on and on cities of hazard that endanger his soul and body, and leaves for others the wealth and wall that he built and that he planted. And a sign for the matter: in the אָלֶ״ף בֵּי״ת the letters of עָנִי ('poor') are adjacent and second to the letters of כֶּסֶף ('silver'), to indicate that every lover of silver is poor in his mind forever. And as for what is said (Psalms 147:2) 'בּוֹנֵה יְרוּשָׁלִַם ה׳' — participle form — it appears one may explain it along the lines of what the Gemara concludes (Bava Batra 75a): the ministering angels saw Him cutting precious stones and pearls, etc. They said to Him: 'These are for whom?' They said to Him: 'The Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to set them in the gates of Jerusalem,' etc. If so, at all times 'ה׳ is building Jerusalem' — and this is easy to understand. Thus the participle 'בּוֹנֶה' said of Cain hints that he was building on and on forever, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 6:2): 'a man to whom Elohim gives wealth and possessions' etc. 'but gives him no power to eat of it.' Now, 'יִתֵּן' is in the future tense, and it should have said 'a man to whom Elohim gave wealth.' Rather, this is its meaning: everyone who is not content with what Elohim has already granted him, and is always seeking that HaShem give him yet more wealth and possessions in addition to what he already has in hand — this one is surely a scoundrel, a miser and a miser, and has no mastery over his own to eat of it — all the more so over what belongs to another."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "The phrase כִּילַי וְקַמְצָן appears to repeat the same concept (miser); rendered literally as two synonymous terms. The reading רֹב יָמָיו הוּא רוֹדֵף אַחַר הָעֹשֶׁר כַּאֲשֶׁר יִרְדֹּף הַקּוֹרֵא בֶּהָרִים is translated hyper-literally; קוֹרֵא may mean a gatherer/forager but the sense is not entirely certain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:15.441296341Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}